/**
 * @ohos/dsh-ontology/vocab — the controlled vocabulary is code, not data.
 *
 * The point of an ontology over a pile of notes is that predicates mean one
 * thing, have one object type, and either replace their predecessor or not.
 * Putting the vocabulary in the source (instead of a `vocabulary` table the
 * model can extend) means the model cannot invent `favoriteColor` and silently
 * create a junk graph; an unknown predicate fails with the list to choose from.
 *
 * Each entry now carries the three things a retrieval layer needs: `about`
 * (what it means), `synonyms` (how a person might say it), and `examples`
 * (what a value looks like). Auto Ontology calls this the Concept layer; here
 * it is still code, but it is *queryable* through the `describe` action instead
 * of being compressed into a tool-description string.
 *
 * `pinned` is the product tier: facts that must be in every request (who the
 * user is, what they cannot eat, what language to answer in). Everything else
 * is queryable but not always injected.
 */

/** @typedef {'text'|'number'|'date'|'bool'|'entity'} ValueKind */

